    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Upconversion materials integrated into semitransparent perovskite solar cells show tremendous potential in\r\nexceeding the single-junction Shockley-Queisser limit of 33% power conversion efficiency (PCE). In order\r\nfor light to reach the upconversion material and be converted into usable wavelengths, highly transparent\r\nand conductive top electrical contacts are needed to replace conventional top contacts. Ultrathin metals that\r\nare ideal for transparent contacts such as gold (Au) have a high surface energy, so seed layers between the\r\ncontact and the hole transport layer are also necessary to promote a continuous surface. In this thesis, I test\r\nmultiple ultrathin top contacts at various thicknesses for use in triple cation lead halide perovskites. These top\r\nelectrical contacts are optimized for compatibility with the triplet-triplet annihilation upconversion materials\r\nused in the Congreve Lab. The primary top contacts tested are MoO3/Au/MoO3 and chromium (Cr) as a\r\nseed layer to Au. A champion efficiency of of 15.3% for the ultrathin contact on a semitransparent perovskite\r\ndevice is achieved, compared to 17.1% for an opaque contact on a perovskite device of the same composition.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hyatt, M. (2024). Transparent Electrical Contacts for use in Semi-Transparent Upconverter Perovskite Solar Cells. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/dr081hx0584. https://doi.org/10.25740/dr081hx0584.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
